216	<p><span class="add"><strong>Where a type declares no <code>=</code>, <code>=</code> is strict equivalence, <code>SEQV</code> (P-168).</strong> Strict equivalence is the operator <code>&#x2263;</code> (STRICTLY EQUIVALENT TO), written <code>SEQV</code> or <code>===</code> in ASCII. The operator <code>&#x2261;</code> (IDENTICAL TO, <code>EQV</code> or <code>EQUIV</code>) is a different one: logical equivalence on booleans. The interpreter's library declares it for no type, and walk stops on it (row 184): write <code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code>. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> answers <code>a SEQV b</code>. <code>SEQV</code> follows the specification on both paths:</span></p>
