235	<p><strong><span class="cut">Under walk a numeral has a run-time class.</span><span class="add">A numeral converts where it stands (P-173).</span></strong> <span class="cut">The checker types an integer numeral as <code>IntLiteral</code>, a sibling under <code>Number</code> that converts to the integer types, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code> (P-76). Walk gives a numeral the class <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> or <code>BigNum</code> by its value. These classes stand for <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, but a generic instantiates at the class: <code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code> (row 364). Walk answers <code>IntLiteral</code>'s arithmetic as a <code>ZZ32</code> (row 578).</span> <span class="add">A numeral's own type is <code>NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]</code>, or <code>RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]</code> with a radix point (P-174). Its static arguments are the numeral's numbers: the digit count, the count of digits after the point, the radix, and the digits read as one integer in that radix, so <code>3.125</code> is a <code>RadixPointNumeral[\4, 3, 10, 3125\]</code>. Both lie below <code>Numeral</code>, a sibling of the number types under <code>Number</code>. No numeral value exists when a program runs, so a numeral type has no getters, no <code>asFloat</code>, no <code>asString</code> and no <code>=</code> with a number type. Until the switch-over, the compiler's prelude follows the same design (P-37). A place fixes a type when its declared type has a coercion from the numeral type, as each of the eight number types has. Such places are a typed binding, an assignment, a field, a parameter and a declared return type. At a number type, the numeral converts from its exact value: exactly into the five integer types and <code>QQ</code>, and with one rounding into <code>RR64</code> and <code>RR32</code> (P-76, P-172). So <code>u: NN32 = 5</code> binds, and <code>q: QQ = 0.1</code> is <code>1/10</code>. A numeral