236	<p><span class="add">Write a numeral type only as the parameter type of a coercion or an operator. The checker refuses it as the type of a variable, a field or a return (P-174). A program's own number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over its numbers, which its body declares, as <code>value object Byte(b: ZZ32)</code> declares <code>coerce[\nat n, nat r, nat v\](x: NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]) where { v &lt; 256 } = Byte(v.asZZ32)</code>. With a radix point, write <code>coerce[\nat n, nat m, nat r, nat v\](x: RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\])</code>. The coercion's body reads <code>n</code>, <code>r</code> and <code>v</code> as <code>ZZ</code> values without a bound, since a numeral's digits fit no fixed width (P-173, P-40). The <code>where</code> clause bounds the numerals that the type takes. The checker refuses statically a numeral that breaks it, as <code>300</code> here: 'The numeral 300 does not meet the where clause of the coercion to Byte.' Walk refuses it when the binding runs. A negative constant, as <code>-3</code>, reaches the type as the type's prefix <code>-</code> on the coercion's result for <code>3</code>, so a type without <code>-</code> refuses it. The coercion gives one value for each place where the numeral is written, and each run of that place reuses it (P-176). Without such a coercion, the type refuses a numeral.</span> The library's factories <code>array1</code> and <code>array2</code> give a <code>Vector</code> or <span class="add">a </span><code>Matrix</code> only at the eight number types<span class="cut">, not at a user subtype of <code>Number</code>. Elsewhere they give a plain array, whose <code>+</code> and unary <code>-</code> fail dispatch under walk. Under walk, an inferred element type is a run-time class, so they give a plain array (row 721). Write the static argument.</span><span class="add">; at a program's own number type they give a plain array. <code>interpreter.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> give the