238	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule, beside a fact that changed. The batch changed how walk prints a type: it now prints its numeral classes <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> and <code>BigNum</code> as <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, also inside a static argument. The old example, &ldquo;<code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code>&rdquo;, named a type that walk no longer prints. A worker who saw <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> in walk's output would take the value for a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>, and a dispatch on that type would then fail. The text now says that the printed name hides the instance. After the cold read, it says what to write where a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> is needed, the workaround that ledger row 364 records. It names the printed names outright, in place of &ldquo;the traits' names&rdquo;. And the sentence on walk's error messages lost a &ldquo;still&rdquo;, which told of a change rather than of what a worker sees.</p>
239	<p><strong>Why, 8 November, afternoon.</strong> Not a new rule: a correction of the rule&rsquo;s text. On 8 November the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them, since they wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language. This item did so with walk&rsquo;s class <code>Int</code> behind a printed <code>ZZ32</code>: its rule told workers what to write because of it. By the curator&rsquo;s rule, <code>revival-changes.md</code> describes the language of revived Fortress, and the revival&rsquo;s changes against the team&rsquo;s design. A behaviour of one path that the language does not give is a defect, with a ledger row, noted in that path&rsquo;s part while it lasts. So the paragraph now says what a numeral is in the language, and its title says so too. The checker types an integer numeral as <code>IntLiteral</code>. Under walk, until walk runs the checker, the specification&rsquo;s revised text gives it <code>ZZ32</code>, or <code>ZZ64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> where its value does not fit. By the curator&rsquo;s decision of the day, P-172, a numeral with a radix point is a <code>FloatLiteral</code> that holds the exact value. <code>QQ</code> converts from it exactly, and <code>RR64</code> and <code>RR32</code> where they are declared, so <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>true</code>. Walk&rsquo;s class <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> or <code>BigNum</code> (row 364), its plain arrays from <code>array1</code> and <code>array2</code> (row 721) and its <code>ZZ32</code> arithmetic on numerals (row 578) moved to <code>interpreter.md</code> (change 42). A cold read then fixed the wording. It states walk&rsquo;s integer numeral in order: first the specification&rsquo;s text, then what walk does today. It makes the pointer to <code>interpreter.md</code> a sentence of its own, with the rows. And it puts back the library&rsquo;s rule on the two factories at a program&rsquo;s own number type, which the first rewrite had moved out.</p>
240	<p><strong>Why, climb batch 29.</strong> A change of the rule, with new rules beside it. The batch built the curator&rsquo;s decisions on numerals, P-173 and P-174. The rule said that a numeral has a type of its own, <code>IntLiteral</code> or <code>FloatLiteral</code>, that under walk an integer numeral is a <code>ZZ32</code> until walk runs the checker, and it pointed to walk&rsquo;s departures (rows 364, 721, 943 and 944). Now a numeral converts where it stands. At a place that fixes a type, as a typed binding, an assignment, a field, a parameter or a declared return type, it converts into that type from its exact value. Elsewhere it takes its default: a <code>ZZ32</code>, or a <code>ZZ64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> where its value does not fit, and an <code>RR64</code> with a radix point. A numeral&rsquo;s own types are now the Working Draft&rsquo;s <code>NaturalNumeral</code> and <code>RadixPointNumeral</code>, and the compiler&rsquo;s prelude keeps the names <code>IntLiteral</code> and <code>FloatLiteral</code> until the switch-over. The batch fixed the four departures, so the pointer to them went. A worker who followed the old rule would have written <code>IntLiteral</code> as a type, which the interpreter&rsquo;s library no longer has, or kept away from forms that now work, such as <code>u: NN32 = 5</code>. The second paragraph is new. It says that a numeral type is written only as the parameter type of a coercion or an operator, how a program&rsquo;s own number type receives a numeral, and that a type without such a coercion refuses one. The rule on <code>array1</code> and <code>array2</code> at a program&rsquo;s own number type stays. The orientation update&rsquo;s own checks added the place whose declared type is a numeral type, <code>Object</code> among the places that take the default, the refusal of a numeral that rounds to an infinity, with walk&rsquo;s <code>Infinity</code> in its place (row 967), and a size of 2^31 or more used as a value (row 962