241	<p><strong>Why, climb batch 29b.</strong> A change of the rule, by the curator&rsquo;s decision on a numeral&rsquo;s type, P-174, which climb batch 29b built on both paths. A numeral&rsquo;s type now exists only at compile time, with the numeral&rsquo;s four numbers as its static arguments, and a number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over those numbers. The old text gave forms that now stop with an error: a coercion <code>coerce(x: NaturalNumeral)</code> whose body reads the getters <code>digits</code>, <code>fractionDigits</code>, <code>radix</code> and <code>digitValue</code>, and the compiled <code>coerce(x: IntLiteral)</code> and <code>coerce(x: FloatLiteral)</code>. It said that a place whose declared type is a numeral type fixes a type, which the rule no longer allows. And its example, that <code>(h = 0.5)</code> is <code>true</code> for <code>h: QQ = 1/2</code>, is <code>false</code> under walk (row 973), so it is gone: change 41 gives the rule&rsquo;s answer. The first paragraph now gives the numeral types with their four numbers, with the example <code>3.125</code>. It says that no numeral value exists when a program runs, so a numeral type has no getters, no <code>asFloat</code>, no <code>asString</code> and no <code>=</code> with a number type, and that the compiler&rsquo;s prelude follows the same design until the switch-over. The second paragraph gives the generic coercion, with the example of <code>Byte</code>, whose <code>where</code> clause bounds the numerals that the type takes. The orientation update made these changes, and its judge said to install them, with a correction here. The text said that the checker does not yet refuse a numeral that breaks the <code>where</code> clause, but the checker&rsquo;s code and its test show that it does, and the text now gives its message. After the skill&rsquo;s cold read, four fixes. &ldquo;A <code>nat</code> or <code>int</code> parameter that is a size&rdquo; named no case, since every such parameter is a size, so a worker could read a coercion&rsquo;s <code>v</code> as a <code>ZZ32</code>. The text now says &ldquo;other than a numeral type&rsquo;s number&rdquo;, and that the coercion&rsquo;s body reads <code>n</code>, <code>r</code> and <code>v</code> as <code>ZZ</code> values without a bound. &ldquo;The digits after the point&rdquo; could mean those digits or their count: it now says &ldquo;the count of digits after the point&rdquo;. The coercion of <code>Byte</code> is now declared in the type&rsquo;s body, where a worker new to Fortress could have declared it beside the type. And the orientation update first gave here the limit of the prelude&rsquo;s <code>RR64</code> and <code>RR32</code>, which take only a numeral with a radix point. That limit stood in three parts of the skill, in three forms, and now stands once, in <code>compiler.md</code> (change 68).</p></div>
