666	<h3><span class="num">Change 82 &middot; tests-writing.md, &ldquo;Where a test goes, and how it passes&rdquo;</span>The key run_exits_nonzero</h3>
667	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
668	<p class="where-in">tests-writing.md, &ldquo;Where a test goes, and how it passes&rdquo;, line 63, the paragraph on when a run fails, before its example: two sentences added before its last words</p>
669	<p>A <code>run</code> fails if the program exits non-zero or a <code>run_*</code> check fails. With no <code>run_out</code> check, its output must contain <code>pass</code> or <code>PASS</code>. <span class="add">With <code>run_exits_nonzero=true</code>, the run must exit non-zero, as a program whose class fails to load does. The <code>run_*</code> checks, and the <code>PASS</code> check where no <code>run_out</code> check is given, still apply.</span> For example:</p>
670	</div></div>
671	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule&rsquo;s meaning: a key that the batch added to the harness. With <code>run_exits_nonzero=true</code> in a <code>.test</code> file, a run passes only if the program exits non-zero, as a program does whose class fails to load. A test of a coercion that raises before <code>run()</code> needs it (change 78). The part&rsquo;s list of keys did not name it, so a worker could not write such a test. The skill writer checked the key in the harness&rsquo;s code. After the cold read, the word &ldquo;instead&rdquo; is gone: it could mean in place of a zero exit only, or in place of the check for <code>PASS</code> too. The text now says that the <code>run_*</code> checks, and the check for <code>PASS</code> where no <code>run_out</code> check is given, still apply, which the writer checked in the same code. How the key works with the <code>XXX</code> prefix stays unwritten, since that code does not settle it.</p></div>
