745	<h2>Words used on this page</h2>
746	<dl class="gloss">
747	<dt>coordinator</dt><dd>Coordinating session.</dd>
748	<dt>diff</dt><dd>Text that shows what a change cut and added.</dd>
749	<dt><code>fortress-repo</code></dt><dd>Skill that holds the rules that bind the workers in the repository.</dd>
750	<dt>skill writer</dt><dd>Headless session that makes every edit of the project skills.</dd>
751	<dt>cold read</dt><dd>Reading of a change by a worker new to the repository, which flags each passage that would send a worker wrong or make it search.</dd>
752	<dt>orientation update</dt><dd>Workflow's rewrite of <code>revival-changes.md</code> after a landing, which a judge checks section by section before the skill writer installs it.</dd>
753	<dt><code>revival-changes.md</code></dt><dd>Part of the <code>fortress-repo</code> skill that states what the revival changed in the team's Fortress, which every task on the tree's code reads whole.</dd>
754	<dt>record-landing workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that lands a batch&rsquo;s results in the record and the skills, and updates this page.</dd>
755	<dt>provenance line</dt><dd>Line of <code>references/sources.md</code> that records where a sentence of the skill came from.</dd>
756	<dt>walk</dt><dd>Fortress interpreter, which runs a program from its syntax tree.</dd>
757	<dt>checker</dt><dd>Static type checker of the compiled path.</dd>
758	<dt>runtime</dt><dd>System that runs a compiled program and holds its values: the class loader, the value classes and the task and transaction code under <code>runtimeSystem/</code>.</dd>
759	<dt>compiled path</dt><dd>Way of running a program through the compiled checker, the code generator and the runtime, as against walk.</dd>
760	<dt>code generator</dt><dd>Part of the compiler that turns a checked program into bytecode.</dd>
761	<dt>switch-over</dt><dd>Planned move of the compiled path onto the interpreter's library.</dd>
762	<dt>interpreter's library</dt><dd>Library under <code>Library/</code> that walk reads, onto which the switch-over moves the compiled path.</dd>
763	<dt>compiler's library, compiler's prelude</dt><dd>Library that the compiled path reads until the switch-over.</dd>
764	<dt>ledger row</dt><dd>Numbered entry of the gap ledger, the list of the known gaps of the tree.</dd>
765	<dt>count stage</dt><dd>Stage of the measure of the distance to the switch-over that checks the interpreter's library with the compiled checker and counts its errors.</dd>
766	<dt>functional method</dt><dd>Method that a call names as a function, with <code>self</code> among its arguments, as an operator of a trait.</dd>
767	<dt>native</dt><dd>Function that the library binds to Java code by a body <code>builtinPrimitive("…")</code>.</dd>
768	<dt>binding</dt><dd>Library declaration whose body names a native's Java code.</dd>
769	<dt>helper</dt><dd>Public static Java method under <code>nativeHelpers/</code> that runs a native on both paths.</dd>
770	<dt>Meet Rule</dt><dd>Overloading rule that asks, for two overloads that neither exclude each other nor nest, a declaration over the meet of their parameter types, the types that both admit.</dd>
771	<dt><code>excludes</code> clause</dt><dd>Clause of a trait that names types that no value of the trait may also have.</dd>
772	<dt><code>comprises</code> clause</dt><dd>Clause of a trait that lists types, one of which every value of the trait has.</dd>
773	<dt><code>where</code> clause</dt><dd>Clause of a declaration that states conditions on its static parameters, as <code>where { v &lt; 256 }</code>.</dd>
774	<dt><code>Number</code></dt><dd>Library's trait above the number types.</dd>
775	<dt>number types</dt><dd>Eight types of the library below <code>Number</code>: <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, integers of 32 bits, 64 bits and any size; <code>NN32</code> and <code>NN64</code>, unsigned integers; <code>QQ</code>, rationals; <code>RR32</code> and <code>RR64</code>, floats of 32 and 64 bits.</dd>
776	<dt><code>Numeral</code></dt><dd>Library&rsquo;s trait above the two numeral types, a sibling of the number types under <code>Number</code>.</dd>
777	<dt>numeral types, <code>NaturalNumeral</code>, <code>RadixPointNumeral</code></dt><dd>Types of a numeral itself, without and with a radix point, below <code>Numeral</code>, which exist only at compile time and take the numeral&rsquo;s numbers as static arguments.</dd>
778	<dt>numeral&rsquo;s numbers</dt><dd>Static arguments of a numeral type: the digit count, the count of digits after the radix point, the radix, and the digits read as one integer in that radix.</dd>
779	<dt>numeral coercion</dt><dd>Coercion from a numeral type, generic over the numeral&rsquo;s numbers, through which a number type takes a numeral.</dd>
780	<dt><code>IntLiteral</code>, <code>FloatLiteral</code></dt><dd>Former types of an integer numeral and of a numeral with a radix point: on both paths until climb batch 29, and in the compiler&rsquo;s prelude until climb batch 29b.</dd>
781	<dt>place that fixes a type</dt><dd>Typed binding, assignment, field, parameter or declared return type whose type has a coercion from the numeral&rsquo;s type, where a numeral converts into that type from its exact value.</dd>
782	<dt>default</dt><dd>Type that a numeral takes where no place fixes one: <code>ZZ32</code>, or <code>ZZ64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> where its value does not fit, and <code>RR64</code> with a radix point.</dd>
783	<dt>fold</dt><dd>Rewrite, before the checker and on both paths, of an expression of literals alone into one literal: a numeral, a quotient of two numerals, a boolean or a string.</dd>
784	<dt>size</dt><dd>Static parameter declared <code>nat</code> or <code>int</code>, whose value is an integer, as an array&rsquo;s length.</dd>
785	<dt><code>reflect</code>, <code>NatParam</code></dt><dd>Library function in <code>NatReflect.fsi</code> that gives a size known only at run time, and the trait of the value that it gives.</dd>
786	<dt>route A, route C</dt><dd>Record&rsquo;s names for the curator&rsquo;s choice of flat number types, and for the way not chosen, a nested number tower.</dd>
787	<dt>task</dt><dd>Unit of work that runs in parallel with others, as each of the two calls in <code>small(n) - small(m)</code> on the compiled path.</dd>
788	<dt><code>Index</code></dt><dd>Library&rsquo;s trait that bounds the index parameter of an indexed type, such as an array, which of the integer types only <code>ZZ32</code> extends.</dd>
789	<dt>radix point</dt><dd>Point between a numeral&rsquo;s whole part and its fraction, as in <code>0.5</code>.</dd>
790	<dt><code>XXX</code> test</dt><dd>Expected-failure test, whose file name starts with <code>XXX</code>, which asserts the right answer and fails until the defect is fixed.</dd>
791	<dt>interim answer</dt><dd>Answer of walk that holds only until walk runs the checker, which no test asserts against.</dd>
792	<dt>coercion</dt><dd>Conversion of a value from one type into another that the library declares, and that a call or a binding applies by itself.</dd>
793	<dt>static argument</dt><dd>Type in <code>[\ \]</code> that instantiates a generic, as <code>ZZ32</code> in <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>.</dd>
794	<dt><code>SEQV</code></dt><dd>Operator of strict equivalence, <code>&#x2263;</code>, also written <code>===</code>, which tells whether two values are the same value.</dd>
795	<dt><code>&#x2261;</code>, <code>EQV</code></dt><dd>Operator of logical equivalence on booleans, which the interpreter's library declares for no type.</dd>
796	<dt>value object</dt><dd>Tuple, <code>()</code>, or object whose declaration, or a trait that it extends, has the modifier <code>value</code>, which <code>SEQV</code> compares field by field.</dd>
797	<dt>reference object</dt><dd>Object that is neither a value object nor a function, which <code>SEQV</code> finds equivalent only to itself.</dd>
798	<dt><code>FlatString</code>, <code>CatString</code></dt><dd>Walk&rsquo;s two kinds of string object: one that holds its characters, and one that joins two strings without copying them, which <code>||</code> builds for a long result.</dd>
799	<dt>glue class</dt><dd>Java class under <code>interpreter/glue/prim/</code> that a binding names, which only walk runs.</dd>
800	<dt>static initializer</dt><dd>Code of a Java class that runs once, when the class loads.</dd>
801	<dt><code>typecase</code></dt><dd>Expression that chooses one of its clauses by the type of its selector&rsquo;s value.</dd>
802	<dt><code>.test</code> file, harness</dt><dd>File that names test programs, with the commands to run on each and the checks of their exits and output; and the Java code that runs those commands and checks.</dd>
803	<dt><code>HasRank</code></dt><dd>Library trait that every array extends.</dd>
804	<dt><code>Equality</code></dt><dd>Library trait that declares <code>=</code>.</dd>
805	<dt>type assumption</dt><dd>Expression <code>e asif T</code>, which has the value of <code>e</code> while the call around it chooses its declaration as if <code>e</code> were a <code>T</code>.</dd>
806	<dt>mark interface</dt><dd>Java interface with no methods, which a class implements so that code can recognize the class.</dd>
807	<dt>footer</dt><dd>Last lines of a commit message, which name Claude as a co-author and give the session&rsquo;s link.</dd>
808	<dt>model</dt><dd>Version of Claude that an agent runs on, named by its tier, such as Opus, or by its identifier, which starts with <code>claude-</code>.</dd>
809	<dt>climb batch</dt><dd>Run of the batch workflow that changes the tree in several parallel tasks and lands their merged work on <code>main</code>, numbered in the record.</dd>
810	<dt>landing</dt><dd>Step that puts a batch&rsquo;s merged work, or the record&rsquo;s update after it, onto <code>main</code>.</dd>
811	<dt>batch review</dt><dd>Report on a climb batch after it lands, with its cost, its conformance and its findings on how the agents worked.</dd>
812	<dt><code>F-</code> and <code>P-</code> numbers</dt><dd>Numbers of the record&rsquo;s entries: <code>F-</code> for a fact of the tree, <code>P-</code> for one of the curator&rsquo;s decisions.</dd>
813	<dt>getter</dt><dd>Method called as a field, with no argument list, as <code>asZZ32</code> in <code>x.asZZ32</code>.</dd>
814	<dt>low-bits function</dt><dd>Function among <code>narrow</code>, <code>widen</code>, <code>signed</code>, <code>unsigned</code> and <code>big</code>, which converts an integer by keeping its low bits, and never raises.</dd>
815	<dt>fixed-width type</dt><dd>Integer type of 32 or 64 bits: <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>NN32</code> or <code>NN64</code>.</dd>
816	<dt><code>IntegerOverflow</code></dt><dd>Library&rsquo;s exception for an integer result that does not fit its type.</dd>
817	<dt><code>FailCalled</code></dt><dd>Library&rsquo;s exception that <code>fail</code> raises, with its message.</dd>
818	<dt>one library</dt><dd>Interpreter&rsquo;s library, as the library of both paths from the switch-over.</dd>
819	<dt><code>atomic</code> block</dt><dd>Block whose reads and writes take effect as one step, as other tasks see them.</dd>
820	<dt>transaction</dt><dd>Run time&rsquo;s record of an <code>atomic</code> block&rsquo;s reads and writes, by which the block takes effect as one step or runs again.</dd>
821	<dt>transfer helper</dt><dd>Java method of a fixed name, through which a binding passes a value that the checker reads as <code>NN32</code>, <code>NN64</code> or <code>Char</code>.</dd>
822	<dt>abbreviated type</dt><dd>Short form of a type, as <code>RR64[3]</code> for an array type.</dd>
823	<dt>shallow clone</dt><dd>Copy of a git repository that holds only its latest commits.</dd>
824	<dt>port</dt><dd>Third-party conversion of the tree to Java 9, which one commit copied onto the team&rsquo;s last commit.</dd>
825	<dt><code>base</code>, <code>change</code>, <code>pass</code></dt><dd>Names of the count stage&rsquo;s timed runs: on the base build, on the change of the checker, and on the change after its second pass, which aims at speed.</dd>
826	<dt>smoke test</dt><dd>Check that catches only a gross fault, as a slowdown of several times.</dd>
827	</dl>
